---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const energyCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '0.5*3*v^2/1000', label: 'kinetic energy (kJ), 3 kg', color: '#ea580c', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: '3*9.81*x/1000', label: 'potential energy (kJ), 3 kg', color: '#0f766e', width: 2.0 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 30, label: 'speed v (m/s) — or height x (m)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 1.5, label: 'energy (kJ)' },
  crossings: true,
  height: 330,
};

const foundationsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Newton\'s second law', note: 'F = ma. The whole of dynamics is this equation applied honestly: find the forces, find the motion.' },
    { math: '2 · Work and energy', note: 'Work = force × distance. Integrating F = ma over distance gives the work–energy theorem: net work equals the change in kinetic energy.' },
    { math: '3 · Conservation when forces are friendly', note: 'Gravity and springs store work reversibly, so kinetic + potential energy stays constant. Friction is the tax collector.' },
    { math: '4 · Momentum for collisions', note: 'Impulse = force × time. Over a collision, momentum is conserved even when energy is not — that is why crumple zones work.' },
    { math: '5 · Rigid bodies add rotation', note: 'The same laws, rotated: torque replaces force, moment of inertia replaces mass, ω replaces v. Every machine is this chapter.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/mechanical-engineering/foundational/mechanics-foundations">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Mechanical engineering is applied Newtonian mechanics: bodies, forces and motion, with heat
    added. Its foundations are small enough to fit on a postcard — F = ma, work–energy, impulse–
    momentum — and deep enough that entire industries run on their consequences. The skill is
    choosing which conservation law makes a problem one line instead of ten.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the chain from F = ma to machines" widget="equationStep" config={foundationsCfg} />

  <h2>Kinetic and potential energy, compared</h2>
  <p>
    Speed is expensive: kinetic energy grows with the <em>square</em> of velocity, so doubling
    speed quadruples the braking distance and the crash energy. Height is linear: every metre of
    lift costs the same. Plot both for a 3 kg mass and the asymmetry is immediate.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="KE grows with v², PE with h — drag and compare" widget="plotter" config={energyCfg} />

  <h2>Why engineers love energy methods</h2>
  <p>
    Force analysis needs details — every contact, every angle. Energy methods skip them: if you
    know the start and end states, the work–energy theorem gives you the answer without the path.
    Roller-coaster heights, flywheel storage, projectile ranges — all one-liners when gravity and
    springs are the only forces that matter.
  </p>

  <h2>From particles to machines</h2>
  <p>
    Real machines are rigid bodies: spinning shafts, swinging arms, meshing gears. Rotational
    mechanics mirrors the linear theory — Iω² for kinetic energy, τ = Iα for acceleration — and
    gears are the gear ratio's promise: trade torque for speed, energy conserved minus friction.
    Thermodynamics enters next, when "energy" starts changing form rather than just moving.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCEES FE Mechanical exam specifications; Hibbeler, <em>Engineering Mechanics</em>; Meriam &amp; Kraige, <em>Dynamics</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
